# LogLine Canon

Status: normative draft  
Canon source: `source/logline.canon.json` and `source/logline.canon.logline`

## 1. Scope

This document defines the normative surface of the LogLine canon.

The canon defines the shape, ordering, branch discipline, and minimum
admissibility rules for LogLine entries. A runtime is an implementation that
claims adherence to this canon. Runtime behavior is not normative unless it is
also stated in this document, the grammar, the schema, or the conformance
corpus.

The key words MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY are to be interpreted
as normative requirement levels.

## 2. Canonical Tuple

A LogLine entry MUST contain exactly these nine positions:

```text
who
did
this
when
confirmed_by
if_ok
if_doubt
if_not
status
```

No implementation MAY add, remove, reorder, or rename a position while claiming
conformance to this canon.

The canonical walk order is:

```text
who -> did -> this -> when -> confirmed_by -> if_ok / if_doubt / if_not -> status
```

All modes, operations, adapters, SDKs, storage engines, optimizers, and user
interfaces MUST preserve this nine-position body.

## 3. Position Semantics

### 3.1 `who`

`who` identifies the accountable origin of the entry.

An implementation MUST preserve the raw value of `who`. An implementation MAY
classify the value as a human, system, runtime, LLM, authority, or domain origin,
but that classification MUST NOT replace the canonical value.

`who` does not confirm evidence and MUST NOT select a branch.

### 3.2 `did`

`did` names the act, movement, operation, or law expressed by the entry.

An implementation MUST preserve the raw value of `did`. An implementation MAY
classify verbs into runtime operations, canonical laws, domain acts, or unknown
acts.

`did` does not execute the act and MUST NOT select a branch.

### 3.3 `this`

`this` names the bounded matter, object, payload, claim, or target.

An implementation MUST preserve the raw value of `this`. A wildcard value `*`
MAY be used only where canon or conformance material permits a wildcard pattern.
In operational contexts, unbounded matter SHOULD resolve to doubt unless the
canon explicitly allows it.

`this` does not fetch external resources and MUST NOT select a branch.

### 3.4 `when`

`when` binds the entry to time, order, phase, sequence, or condition.

An implementation MUST preserve the raw value of `when`. It MAY parse absolute
timestamps, windows, phases, wildcards, and relative terms. Relative time such as
`tomorrow` MAY be recognized, but MUST NOT be silently converted into an
absolute ledger time without explicit context.

`when` does not schedule work and MUST NOT release consequence.

### 3.5 `confirmed_by`

`confirmed_by` is the admissibility pivot.

An implementation MUST preserve the raw value of `confirmed_by`. It MAY resolve
evidence tokens, receipts, digests, signatures, quorum declarations, or local
provided evidence.

Only `confirmed_by` may collapse evidential admissibility into:

```text
ok
doubt
not
```

`confirmed_by = none` MUST NOT be treated as confirmed success by omission. It
MUST resolve to doubt, rejection, or an explicitly canon-permitted unconfirmed
state.

Parsing an evidence requirement is not the same as verifying it:

```text
signature:key_123 parsed != signature verified
receipt:ev_123 parsed   != receipt trusted
digest:abc parsed       != digest matched
quorum:2:ops parsed     != quorum satisfied
```

An implementation MUST NOT invent proof, fetch remote proof implicitly, or
fabricate receipts while resolving `confirmed_by`.

### 3.6 `if_ok`

`if_ok` names the governed route when `confirmed_by` resolves ok.

`if_ok` MAY name a release route, continuation, emission, compilation, tower
release, status commit, or domain route. It MUST NOT prove evidence. It MUST NOT
execute consequence merely by being selected.

### 3.7 `if_doubt`

`if_doubt` names the governed route when `confirmed_by` resolves doubt or when
the entry is structurally admissible but not sufficiently resolved.

`if_doubt` MAY name clarification, suspension, ghost handling, return to LogLine,
halt, or open-state marking. It MUST NOT hide doubt by converting it into ok.

### 3.8 `if_not`

`if_not` names the governed route when the entry is inadmissible, contradicted,
forbidden, or falsified.

`if_not` MAY name rejection, refusal, denial, rollback label, voiding, failed
marking, or prohibition. It MUST NOT execute rollback machinery merely by being
selected.

### 3.9 `status`

`status` records lifecycle truth after the governed walk.

`status` MAY compute lifecycle transitions and MAY expose an append-only ledger
boundary. It MUST NOT create evidence, execute branch consequence, repair failed
side effects, or persist global state without an explicit ledger call.

Status closes truth only from walked evidence.

## 4. Textual `.logline` Form

The textual form MUST follow `spec/grammar.ebnf`.

For a normal LogLine entry, the first four positions are positional:

```text
<who> <did> <this> <when>
```

The final five positions MUST be introduced by explicit markers:

```text
confirmed_by <value> if_ok <value> if_doubt <value> if_not <value> status <value>
```

Therefore the canonical textual shape is:

```text
who did this when confirmed_by evidence if_ok ok_route if_doubt doubt_route if_not not_route status lifecycle
```

The `form require` directive is a canon declaration and MUST require the nine
canonical positions in canonical order.

## 5. JSON Canon Form

The JSON canon artifact MUST validate against `spec/logline-canon.schema.json`.

A canon JSON object MUST include:

```text
canon
version
form
runtime_walk
route_kinds
status_lifecycle
law
prohibition
```

`runtime_walk` MUST contain exactly the nine positions in canonical order.

`law` and `prohibition` entries MUST be represented as nine-field LogLine
objects.

## 6. Route Classes

The canon defines four route classes:

```text
ok
doubt
not
none
```

Each branch position MUST resolve to a route token whose meaning is declared by
the canon, a domain extension, or an explicit conformance case.

The route classes in the source canon are:

```text
ok:
  enter_translation
  emit_logline
  enter_strong_grammar
  emit_ir
  enter_runtime_walk
  release_to_tower
  execute
  commit_status
  close

doubt:
  ask_or_suspend
  ghost_and_clarify
  remain_unreleased
  return_to_logline
  suspend
  halt
  mark_open

not:
  reject
  refuse_translation
  void
  reject_shape
  deny
  do_not_execute
  rollback
  mark_failed
  forbid

none:
  none
```

`none` is a route class, not proof. It MUST NOT cause evidence success by
absence.

## 7. Lifecycle

The lifecycle transitions are:

```text
draft       -> pending, void
pending     -> confirmed, doubt, rejected
doubt       -> pending, rejected, void
confirmed   -> released, rejected
released    -> closed, failed, rolled_back
failed      -> rolled_back, closed
rolled_back -> closed
rejected    -> closed
void        -> closed
closed      -> []
```

An implementation MUST reject, doubt, or explicitly classify transitions not
allowed by the canon version it claims.

`rolled_back` is a lifecycle label. It is not rollback machinery.

## 8. Prohibitions

The canon includes prohibition entries. A prohibition MAY use `*` or the legacy
time wildcard alias `any_time` as pattern material.

An implementation MUST NOT allow a prohibited entry to release consequence. A
matching prohibition SHOULD resolve through `if_not` and produce a not branch or
equivalent rejection report.

The prohibition intent includes:

```text
natural_language MUST NOT execute before LogLine
llm MUST NOT become automation by itself
act MUST NOT bypass LogLine
act MUST NOT release without confirmation
```

## 9. Conformance Levels

Implementations MAY declare conformance by level:

```text
Level 0: Parse textual `.logline`
Level 1: Validate JSON schema
Level 2: Apply semantic rules and conformance corpus
Level 3: Produce and verify receipts
Level 4: Maintain append-only verifiable ledger
```

Claims MUST name the canon version and conformance suite version.

## 10. Runtime Boundary

The reference Rust runtime is an implementation. It is not the source of the
norm.

The authority chain is:

```text
canon -> conformance corpus -> receipts -> implementation report
```

The CLI may parse, print, and dispatch. It MUST NOT become a tenth runtime
field. Any crate, module, or subsystem that participates in the runtime walk
MUST preserve the nine-position anatomy.
